  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nHurricane Marie remains on a track toward the west-northwest with \nthe center to remain well to the southwest of the area through the \nholiday weekend. The current NHC forecast has Marie weakening to a \ntropical storm on Sunday. The main impacts expected for Southern \nCalifornia over the weekend are increased surf, coastal flooding, \nand periods of light to locally moderate precipitation.  \n\nMid to high level moisture from Marie will begin to increase from \nthe south tonight into Saturday, increasing chances of showers \nfor San Diego County. The moisture will continue to shift north \nSaturday into Sunday, increasing precipitation chances for Orange,\nRiverside, and San Bernardino Counties. For much of the area the \nmost likely peak rainfall rates will be 0.20-0.40 inches per hour.\nThere is a slight chance (10-15%) of thunderstorms over the San \nBernardino mountains and High Desert Sunday afternoon. Current \nguidance indicates we'll be on the edge of the bulk of the \ninstability. If the high cloud coverage is widespread enough to \nlimit heating from the sun any afternoon convection may be \nthwarted. \n\nFor this weekend, high temperatures will remain below average inland \nwith highs near the coast running a few degrees above average. \nOvernight lows will also return to above average for this time of \nyear by Sunday. By Wednesday, high temperatures will be within a few \ndegrees of average for the mountains and deserts and a few to around \n5 degrees above average for the coast and valleys. Above average low \ntemperatures will be maintained into next weekend. High temperatures \nwill gradually cool into next Friday. \n\nThe upper level flow turns south/southeasterly Wednesday and \nThursday, which may allow for some additional moisture to creep into \nthe area. There remains some uncertainty in the upper level pattern \nwith the position of the upper level ridge over the southern US \nand a trough off the US West Coast. A more easterly position of \nthe trough would result in drier conditions with a more westerly \nposition of the ridge allowing more moisture into the area. \nCurrent forecast follows the NBM which has 20 to 35 percent chance\nof precipitation over the mountains Wednesday and Thursday \nafternoon and a slight chance of thunderstorms.",
